{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And when you sell, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: From where do we know that there is no ona'ah (fraud) for land? Scripture says: ״אוֹ קָנֹה מִיַּד — אַל תּוֹנוּ״ — movable goods are subject to ona'ah but there is no ona'ah for land, thus far. And in the Gemara (Bava Metzia 56a) they raised a difficulty from the verse ״אֶת כָּל אַרְצוֹ מִיָּדוֹ״, and they resolved: ״מִיָּדוֹ״ — his hand literally, and there it is different, for one cannot say thus, thus far. It is difficult: do we then learn from there that whenever it says ״יָדוֹ״ it does not mean his hand literally? And one may answer that the word ״יָדוֹ״ does mean his hand literally, except that it can also be understood as his domain; but the meaning of יָדוֹ as his actual hand is the more proximate reading. Therefore when Scripture says ״יָדוֹ״, even though both interpretations are valid in it, I judge only according to the more proximate level, which is his hand. And if the intention of Scripture were not to say his hand literally, it would be incumbent upon it not to use a word that we could interpret with an alien meaning — unlike a case where a word can bear only one interpretation, where Scripture would not be concerned about using language in which the matter could be heard differently. Yet it remains for us to investigate this according to our Rabbis of blessed memory, who derived that the warning of ״אַל תּוֹנוּ״ applies only to movable goods — when surely the main thrust of the verse is stated regarding the sale of land." "<b>And it appears</b> that although our Rabbis of blessed memory derived thus, it was according to the halakhot that they had in hand from Sinai, for which they found support in the verses. And in any event the verse may be explained according to its plain sense in this manner: for inasmuch as HaShem commanded concerning the land that it not be sold in perpetuity, He commanded that the seller not defraud the buyer who is not expert in the laws of the Torah — for the land is to be his and his seed's forever, and afterward he will come with the claim of a royal decree to restore it to him. And a matter analogous to this ona'ah is also justified in the case of land: one who sells land on the condition that there are no contesting claimants against it, and contesting claimants emerge against it — that is a nullification of the sale. And here you have no contesting claimant like the seller himself, who comes back to contest his field to restore it to himself under the claim of the Torah's right. Therefore HaShem commanded and said: ״בְּמִסְפַּר שָׁנִים וגו׳ יִמְכָּר לָךְ״. But in that Scripture says ״אַל תּוֹנוּ״ without specification, encompassing every kind of ona'ah — that applies only to movable goods, as Scripture precisely says ״קָנֹה מִיַּד״, according to the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:06.588192784Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}